able expectation of privacy in the place of trespass and therefore cannot invoke the Fourth Amendment to suppress evidence found there; reaffirms Rakas 'standing'-as-merits framing (legitimate expectation of privacy, no automatic standing).\",\"doctrine_node\":\"exclusionary.standing / Rakas reasonable-expectation-of-privacy (capacity to challenge)\",\"discovery_path\":\"WebSearch 'published circuit court 2025 Fourth Amendment standing capacity to challenge search reasonable expectation privacy' -> CaseMine commentary on Parkerson (5th Cir. 2025)\",\"confidence\":\"high\",\"lane\":\"TW-CLAUDE-2\",\"model\":\"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/s9/p4/out/TW-CLAUDE-2-candidates.jsonl:2:{\"row\":\"p4.tw.v1\",\"unit\":\"exclusionary\",\"caption\":\"United States v. Gonzalez-Arocho\",\"court\":\"1st Cir.\",\"date\":\"2025\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"1st Cir. No. 25-1041 (published)\",\"proposition\":\"Good-faith exception rejected where HSI agents knowingly seized and searched an iPhone different from the device specified in the warrant, exceeding the warrant's scope; government failed its 'heavy burden' to show objective good faith, so suppression was affirmed.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"exclusionary.good-faith-exception (Leon/Herring; warrant-scope overreach)\",\"discovery_path\":\"WebSearch '2024 2025 federal appeals court good faith exception ... warrant suppression' + caption-confirm search -> ca1.uscourts.gov 25-1041P-01A.pdf\",\"confidence\":\"high\",\"lane\":\"TW-CLAUDE-2\",\"model\":\"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/s9/p4/out/TW-CLAUDE-2-candidates.jsonl-3-{\"row\":\"p4.tw.v1\",\"unit\":\"exclusionary\",\"caption\":\"Chatrie v. United States\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2026-06-29\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 25-112, 607 U.S. ___ (2026)\",\"proposition\":\"Cell-phone location (geofence Location History) data is protected by the Fourth Amendment and its acquisition is a search requiring a warrant; the 4th Cir. en banc had denied suppression on the good-faith exception, so the case also carries exclusionary-rule (Leon good-faith) content. PRIMARILY the digital-surveillance umbrella (an I5 unit that already ran this phase) and reportedly already in the corpus (Chatrie/Zorn cluster-id item); listed low-confidence only for its standing/REP + good-faith crossover to this unit.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"exclusionary.good-faith-exception + standing/REP (crossover from digital third-party-doctrine)\",\"discovery_path\":\"WebSearch 'Supreme Court 2025 term Fourth Amendment ... suppression ruling' + 'United States v Ray Fourth Circuit 2025 phone search warrant good faith' -> supremecourt.gov 25-112 opinion, Bloomberg/Conversation coverage\",\"confidence\":\"low\",\"lane\":\"TW-CLAUDE-2\",\"model\":\"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/s9/p4/out/TW-CLAUDE-2-candidates.jsonl-4-{\"row\":\"p4.tw.v1\",\"unit\":\"confessions\",\"caption\":\"United States v. Pence\",\"court\":\"2d Cir.\",\"date\":\"2026-04-10\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"2d Cir. No. 24-1025-cr (published)\",\"proposition\":\"A murder-for-hire defendant questioned for over two hours in an FBI vehicle outside his home while armed agents executed a dawn search warrant was NOT in custody for Miranda purposes (chose to enter vehicle, unhandcuffed, told he was not under arrest, not threatened, never asked to leave); denial of suppression of pre-warning statements affirmed as 'a close call.'\",\"doctrine_node\":\"confessions.miranda-custody (reasonable-person / free-to-terminate)\",\"discovery_path\":\"WebSearch '2025 published federal circuit court Miranda custody interrogation waiver invocation suppression confession' + Pence caption-confirm -> FindLaw/NYDailyRecord/Justia (Chin, J.)\",\"confidence\":\"high\",\"lane\":\"TW-CLAUDE-2\",\"model\":\"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n--\n_run/s9/p4/out/SMP-S7-sample.jsonl-26-{\"row\": \"p4.sample.v1\", \"source_row\": \"R9 fix-list disposition audit: R9 'community caretaking reaches persons' (S7-CHANGELIST item 26)\", \"verdict\": \"CONFIRM\", \"basis\": \"REFUTED-BY-RESEARCH land