{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -o '.{0,100}(702|703).{0,100}' _run/s9/p4/star-refetch/145922.html | head -40\"","aggregated_output":">107 S.Ct. 1160</a></span> (quoting <i>United States v. Peltier,</i> <span class=\"star-pagination\">*702</span> <span class=\"citation\" data-id=\"9426173\"><a href=\"/opinion/109302/united-states-v-peltier/#5\n<p><span class=\"star-pagination\">*703</span> Our decision in <i>Franks v. Delaware,</i> <span class=\"citation\" data-id=\"9427321\"><a href=\not raised by an isolated, negligent recordkeeping error attenuated from the arrest. <i>Ante,</i> at 702, 704.<sup>[2]</sup> In my view, the Court's opinion underestimates the need for a forceful exclusio\nrrence that outweighs the cost to the justice system, <i>ante,</i> at 704. See also <i>ante,</i> at 702 (\"[T]he exclusionary rule serves to deter deliberate, reckless, or grossly negligent conduct, or in\ne rule to \"evidence obtained by flagrant or deliberate violation of rights.\" <i>Ibid.; ante,</i> at 702.</p>\r\ne when the misconduct at issue is merely careless, not intentional or reckless. See <i>ante,</i> at 702, 703. The suggestion runs counter to a foundational premise of tort lawthat liability for negligen\n<p>Is the potential deterrence here worth the costs it imposes? See <i>ante,</i> at 702. In light of the paramount importance of accurate recordkeeping in law enforcement, I would answer \n have knowingly made false entries to lay the groundwork for future false arrests.\" <i>Ante,</i> at 703. This concession provides little comfort.</p>\r\n3</a></span> (Brennan, J., dissenting). In keeping with the rule's \"core concerns,\" <i>ante,</i> at 702, suppression should have attended the unconstitutional search in this case.</p>\r\nexclusionary rule does not require inquiry into the mental state of the police. See <i>ante,</i> at 703; <i>Whren v. United States,</i> <span class=\"citation\" data-id=\"118036\"><a href=\"/opinion/118036/wh\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
