n whether he had permission to be on the public land. Applying the two-part *Katz* test (subjective expectation society recognizes as reasonable), the court held:\",\ncontent/cases/United States v. Leary.md:13:authority_weight: \"Binding in-circuit — 10th Cir.\"\ncontent/cases/United States v. Leary.md:43:*846 F.2d 592 (10th Cir. 1988)* · U.S. Court of Appeals, Tenth Circuit · **Binding in-circuit — 10th Cir.** · Treatment: **good** *(as of 2026-06-30)*\ncontent/cases/United States v. Leary.md:68:- **Status:** good *(as of 2026-06-30)* — **Binding in-circuit — 10th Cir.**\ncontent/cases/Knight v. Jacobson.md:13:authority_weight: \"Binding in-circuit — 11th Cir.\"\ncontent/cases/Knight v. Jacobson.md:55:*300 F.3d 1272 (11th Cir. 2002)* (No. 01-15506) · U.S. Court of Appeals for the Eleventh Circuit · **Binding in-circuit — 11th Cir.** · Treatment: **Unverified**\n_overhaul2/lake/cases/United States v. Gooch.json:142:      \"quote\": \"--- # United States v. Gooch *6 F.3d 673 (9th Cir. 1993)* \\u00b7 U.S. Court of Appeals, Ninth Circuit \\u00b7 **Binding in-circuit \\u2014 9th Cir.** \\u00b7 Treatment: **good** *(as of 2026-06-30)* <!-- header line; TreatmentBadge + weight render here, degrading to the text above --> ## Background Responding to a 3:50 a.m. report that a man had fired a shot at a state campground, Stevens County officers located Kenneth Gooch \\u2014 who had been living in a closed tent there for several days with no other residence \\u2014 asleep in his tent. Without an arrest warrant, they ordered him out, arrested and handcuffed him, locked him in a patrol car 20 yards away, removed the other occupant, and then, still without a warrant, searched the tent and found a loaded handgun under his air mattress. A post-trial suppression hearing held the firearm should have been suppressed; the government appealed. ## Issue Whether a person has a Fourth Amendment reasonable expectation of privacy in a closed tent pitched on a public campground, such that a warrantless search of the tent violates the Fourth Amendment. ## Rule Yes. A tent is treated as a dwelling for Fourth Amendment purposes, not as a vehicle. Occupancy of a tent requires\",\n_run/o2-execute/w6-payloads/payne.md:24:*99 F.4th 495 (9th Cir. 2024)* (No. 22-50262) · U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit · **Binding in-circuit — 9th Cir.** · Treatment: **Unverified**\n_run/o2-execute/w6-payloads/meyer.md:23:*19 F.4th 1028 (8th Cir. 2021)* (No. 20-2958) · U.S. Court of Appeals for the Eighth Circuit · **Binding in-circuit — 8th Cir.** · Treatment: **Unverified**\ncontent/cases/United States v. Soto-Peguero.md:13:authority_weight: \"Binding in-circuit — 1st Cir.\"\ncontent/cases/United States v. Soto-Peguero.md:43:*978 F.3d 13 (1st Cir. 2020)* · U.S. Court of Appeals, First Circuit · **Binding in-circuit — 1st Cir.** · Treatment: **good** *(as of 2026-06-30)*\ncontent/cases/United States v. Soto-Peguero.md:62:- **Status:** good *(as of 2026-06-30)* — **Binding in-circuit — 1st Cir.**\n_run/o2-execute/w6-payloads/perez.md:23:*89 F.4th 247 (1st Cir. 2023)* (No. 22-1121) · U.S. Court of Appeals for the First Circuit · **Binding in-circuit — 1st Cir.** · Treatment: **Unverified**\n_overhaul2/lake/cases/United States v. Touset.json:66:      \"quote\": \"--- # United States v. Touset *890 F.3d 1227 (11th Cir. 2018)* \\u00b7 U.S. Court of Appeals, Eleventh Circuit \\u00b7 **Binding in-circuit \\u2014 11th Cir.** \\u00b7 Treatment: **good** *(as of 2026-06-30)* <!-- header line; TreatmentBadge + weight render here, degrading to the text above --> ## Background Customs agents at the border forensically searched Touset's electronic devices and found child pornography. The investigation had begun with information that Touset made small Western Union payments to an entity in the Philippines (a country associated with child exploitation) that used an email address tied to child pornography. Touset moved to suppress, and the district court \\u2014 following the Ninth Circuit's *Cotterman* \\u2014 held that reasonable suspicion was required and was present. ## Issue Whether the Fourth Amendment requires any suspicion \\u2014 in particular, reasonable suspicion \\u2014 for a forensic search of electronic devices at the international border. ## Rule No. The border-search exception requires no suspicion for searches of property at the border, and that rule extends to forensic searches of electronic devices:\",\ncontent/cases/Alvarez v. City of Brownsville.md:13:authority_weight: \"Binding in-circuit — 5th Cir.\"\ncontent/cases/Alvarez v. City of Brownsville.md:52:*904 F.3d 382 (5th Cir. 2018)* (en banc) (No. 16-40772) · U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit · **Binding in-circuit — 5th Cir.** · Treatment: **Unverified**\n_run/o2-execute/w6-payloads/vasquez-algarin.md:23:*821 F.3d 467 (3d Cir. 2016)* (No. 15-1941) · U.S. Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit · **Binding in-circuit — 3d Cir.** · Treatment: **Unverified**\ncontent/cases/State v. Demesme.md:13:authority_weight: \"Persuasive — state, illustrative\"\ncontent/cases/State v. Demesme.md:51:*228 So. 3d 1206 (La. 2017)* (No. 2017-KK-0954) · Supreme Court of Louisiana · **Persuasive — state, illustrative** · Treatment: **Unverified**\n_run/o2-execute/w6-payloads/xiang.md:24:*67 F.4th 895 (8th Cir. 2023)* (No. 22-1801) · U.S. Court of Appeals for the Eighth Circuit · **Binding in-circuit — 8th Cir.** · Treatment: **Unverified**\n_run/o2-execute/w6-payloads/moore-bush.md:27:*36 F.4th 320 (1st Cir. 2022) (en banc)* (No. 19-1582) · U.S. Court of Appeals for the First Circuit · **Binding in-circuit — 1st Cir.** · Treatment: **Unverified**\n_run/o2-execute/w6-payloads/wilson.md:24:*13 F.4th 961 (9th Cir. 2021)* (No. 18-50440) · U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit · **Binding in-circuit — 9th Cir.** · Treatment: **Unverified**\n_run/o2-execute/w6-payloads/oliveras.md:23:*96 F.4th 298 (2d Cir. 2024)* (No. 21-2954) · U.S. Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit · **Bindin