oundations-and-the-fourth-amendment/Fourth Amendment Recalibration.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Fourth Amendment Recalibration\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"foundations-and-the-fourth-amendment/Fourth Amendment Recalibration.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"Milam v. United States\", \"cite\": \"296 F. 629 (1924)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"The meaning of 'unreasonable searches' is not fixed but changes with social, economic, and legal conditions; on that reasoning the warrantless stop and search of a truck (which turned up smuggled persons rather than the expected liquor) was not unreasonable and the evidence was competent.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/Milam v. United States.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:42:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"state-v-christensen--4381703\", \"record_id_after\": \"State v. Christensen\", \"caption\": \"State v. Christensen\", \"cite\": \"2017 Tenn. LEXIS 195 (2017)\", \"year\": 2017, \"court\": \"Tenn.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Persuasive — state, illustrative\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4381703/state-of-tennessee-v-james-robert-christensen-jr/\", \"holding\": \"'No Trespassing' signs posted near an unobstructed driveway do not, by themselves, revoke the implied license that permits police to approach a home and conduct a knock-and-talk; only barriers like a fence and closed gate that physically block access will do so.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"warrant-exceptions/Knock and Talk.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/Knock and Talk.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Knock and Talk\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/Knock and Talk.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"State v. Christensen\", \"cite\": \"2017 Tenn. LEXIS 195 (2017)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"'No Trespassing' signs posted near an unobstructed driveway do not, by themselves, revoke the implied license that permits police to approach a home and conduct a knock-and-talk; only barriers like a fence and closed gate that physically block access will do so.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/State v. Christensen.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:43:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"state-v-demesme--5035127\", \"record_id_after\": \"State v. Demesme\", \"caption\": \"State v. Demesme\", \"cite\": \"228 So. 3d 1206 (2017)\", \"year\": 2017, \"court\": \"La. 2017\", \"authority_weight\": \"Persuasive — state, illustrative\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/5035127/state-v-demesme/\", \"holding\": \"The Louisiana Supreme Court denied review of a suppression ruling; in a solo concurrence, Justice Crichton wrote that the defendant's ambiguous reference to a 'lawyer dog' was not an unambiguous request for counsel under Davis v. United States and so did not require officers to stop questioning.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"confessions-interrogation-and-the-fifth-amendment/Miranda Waiver and Invocation.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"confessions-interrogation-and-the-fifth-amendment/Miranda Waiver and Invocation.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Miranda Waiver and Invocation\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"confessions-interrogation-and-the-fifth-amendment/Miranda Waiver and Invocation.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"State v. Demesme\", \"cite\": \"228 So. 3d 1206 (2017)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"The Louisiana Supreme Court denied review of a suppression ruling; in a solo concurrence, Justice Crichton wrote that the defendant's ambiguous reference to a 'lawyer dog' was not an unambiguous request for counsel under Davis v.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/State v. Demesme.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:44:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"state-v-karston--1767998\", \"record_id_after\": \"State v. Karston\", \"caption\": \"State v. Karston\", \"cite\": \"588 So. 2d 165 (1991)\", \"year\": 1991, \"court\": \"La. Ct. App. 1991\", \"authority_weight\": \"Persuasive — state, illustrative\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/1767998/state-v-karston/\", \"holding\": \"A tenant has a reasonable expectation of privacy in the fenced, gated common courtyard of a private apartment complex; an officer who, without probable cause or reasonable suspicion, opens a closed (though unlocked) gate and enters that courtyard to establish surveillance conducts an unreasonable search, so evidence derived from the resulting warrant must be suppressed.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"searches/Curtilage.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"searches/Curtilage.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Curtilage\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"searches/Curtilage.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"State v. Karston\", \"cite\": \"588 So. 2d 165 (1991)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"A tenant has a reasonable expectation of privacy in the fenced, gated common courtyard of a private apartment complex; an officer who, without probable cause or reasonable suspicion, opens a closed (though unlocked) gate and enters that courtyard to establish surveillance conducts an unreasonable search, so evidence derived from the resulting warrant must be suppressed.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/State v. Karston.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:45:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"state-v-weaver--2546485\", \"record_id_after\": \"State v. Weaver\", \"caption\": \"State v. Weaver\", \"cite\": \"2011 Tex. Crim. App. LEXIS 1320 (2011)\", \"year\": 2011, \"court\": \"Tex. Crim. App.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Persuasive — state, illustrative\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/2546485/state-v-weaver/\", \"holding\": \"A canine sniff of a vehicle on private, non-public business premises exceeds the scope of a limited consent and is unlawful once the owner's consent to be there for a particular purpose has ended — the rule that a dog sniff is not itself a Fourth Amendment search presupposes that the officer, and therefore the dog, has a lawful right to be where the sniff occurs, so suppression was proper where officers lingered and deployed a drug dog after their consented-to search for a person came up empty.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"searches/Curtilage.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"searches/Curtilage.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Curtilage\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"searches/Curtilage.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"State v. Weaver\", \"cite\": \"2011 Tex. Crim. App. LEXIS 1320 (2011)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"A canine sniff of a vehicle on private, non-public business premises exceeds the scope of a limited consent and is unlawful once the owner's consent to be there for a particular purpose has ended — the rule that a dog sniff is not itself a Fourth Amendment search presupposes that the officer, and therefore the dog, has a lawful right to be where the sniff occurs, so suppression was proper where officers lingered and deployed a drug dog after their consented-to search for a person came up empty.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/State v. Weaver.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:46:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"state-v-wint--8267547\", \"record_id_after\": \"State v. Wint\", \"caption\": \"State v. Wint\", \"cite\": \"236 N.J. 174 (2018)\", \"year\": 2018, \"court\": \"N.J. 2018\", \"authority_weight\": \"Persuasive — state, illustrative\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/8267547/state-v-wint/\", \"holding\": \"A suspect's six months of continuous pre-indictment custody is not a 'break in custody' under Maryland v. Shatzer, so the Edwards bar on police-initiated reinterrogation after an invocation of counsel remained in force; because none of the three exceptions — counsel provided, defendant-initiated communication, or a break in custody — applied, a later Mirandized waiver could not validate the interrogation and the incriminating statements had to be suppressed.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"confessions-interrogation-and-the-fifth-amendment/Miranda Waiver and Invocation.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"confessions-interrogation-and-the-fifth-amendment/Miranda Waiver and Invocation.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Miranda Waiver and Invocation\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"confessions-interrogation-and-the-fifth-amendment/Miranda Waiver and Invocation.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"State v. Wint\", \"cite\": \"236 N.J. 174 (2018)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"A suspect's six months of continuous pre-indictment custody is not a 'break in custody' under Maryland v.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/State v. Wint.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:47:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-aigbekaen--4680725\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Aigbekaen\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Aigbekaen\", \"cite\": \"943 F.3d 713 (2019)\", \"year\": 2019, \"court\": \"4th Cir. 2019\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 4th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4680725/united-states-v-raymond-aigbekaen/\", \"holding\": \"The border-search exception does not authorize a warrantless, nonroutine forensic search of a returning traveler's electronic devices unless the government's individualized suspicion bears some nexus to the exception's historic purposes — protecting national security, collecting duties, blocking unwanted entrants, or intercepting contraband; suspicion of purely domestic crimes is not enough, so the forensic searches of Aigbekaen's devices violated the Fourth Amendment, though the good-faith exception barred suppression.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"warrant-exceptions/programmatic-and-special-needs-searches/Border Searches.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/programmatic-and-special-needs-searches/Border Searches.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Border Searches\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/programmatic-and-special-needs-searches/Border Searches.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Aigbekaen\", \"cite\": \"943 F.3d 713 (2019)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"The border-search exception does not authorize a warrantless, nonroutine forensic search of a returning traveler's electronic devices unless the government's individualized suspicion bears some nexus to the exception's historic purposes — protecting national security, collecting duties, blocking unwanted entrants, or intercepting contraband; suspicion of purely domestic crimes is not enough, so the forensic searches of Aigbekaen's devices violated the Fourth Amendment, though the good-faith exception barred suppression.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Aigbekaen.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:48:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-amos--9452158\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Amos\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Amos\", \"cite\": \"88 F.4th 446 (2023)\", \"year\": 2023, \"court\": \"3d Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 3d Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/9452158/united-states-v-shiheem-amos/\", \"holding\": \"A show of authority does not effect a Fourth Amendment seizure unless the suspect actually submits to it; a suspect already in motion who raises his hands only partway and pauses momentarily before fleeing has not submitted, so no seizure occurs until he is physically restrained — meaning a handgun that falls at that point is not the fruit of any earlier, unsupported seizure.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"seizures/Seizure of the Person.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/Seizure of the Person.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Seizure of the Person\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/Seizure of the Person.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Amos\", \"cite\": \"88 F.4th 446 (2023)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"A show of authority does not effect a Fourth Amendment seizure unless the suspect actually submits to it; a suspect already in motion who raises his hands only partway and pauses momentarily before fleeing has not submitted, so no seizure occurs until he is physically restrained — meaning a handgun that falls at that point is not the fruit of any earlier, unsupported seizure.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Amos.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:49:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-berkowitz--557342\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Berkowitz\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Berkowitz\", \"cite\": \"927 F.2d 1376 (1991)\", \"year\": 1991, \"court\": \"7th Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 7th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/557342/united-states-v-marvin-berkowitz/\", \"holding\": \"Payton's warrant requirement for in-home arrests bars only a warrantless, nonexigent physical entry into the home, not an officer's announcement of an arrest from outside the threshold; a warrantless arrest is therefore valid where police announce it from outside and the suspect surrenders at the doorway, but unlawful where police enter the home before effecting the arrest — so a suppression claim turning on which occurred requires an evidentiary hearing.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"seizures/arrests/Arrest in the Home.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/arrests/Arrest in the Home.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Arrest in the Home\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/arrests/Arrest in the Home.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Berkowitz\", \"cite\": \"927 F.2d 1376 (1991)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"Payton's warrant requirement for in-home arrests bars only a warrantless, nonexigent physical entry into the home, not an officer's announcement of an arrest from outside the threshold; a warrantless arrest is therefore valid where police announce it from outside and the suspect surrenders at the doorway, but unlawful where police enter the home before effecting the arrest — so a suppression claim turning on which occurred requires an evidentiary hearing.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Berkowitz.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:50:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-black--821235\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Black\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Black\", \"cite\": \"707 F.3d 531 (2013)\", \"year\": 2013, \"court\": \"U.S. Court of Appeals, 4th Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 4th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/821235/united-states-v-nathaniel-black/\", \"holding\": \"Reasonable suspicion for a Terry stop cannot be built by patching together a set of innocent, suspicion-free facts; a companion's lawful open carry of a firearm, another person's arrest history, presence in a high-crime area at night, and cooperative behavior — none particularized to the defendant — do not add up to reasonable suspicion, and there is no reasonable suspicion merely by association.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"seizures/Terry Stops and Reasonable Suspicion.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/Terry Stops and Reasonable Suspicion.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Terry Stops and Reasonable Suspicion\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/Terry Stops and Reasonable Suspicion.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Black\", \"cite\": \"707 F.3d 531 (2013)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"Reasonable suspicion for a Terry stop cannot be built by patching together a set of innocent, suspicion-free facts; a companion's lawful open carry of a firearm, another person's arrest history, presence in a high-crime area at night, and cooperative behavior — none particularized to the defendant — do not add up to reasonable suspicion, and there is no reasonable suspicion merely by association.\"}], \"worklist_note\": \"2026-07-07 orchestrator remap post-re-key: united-states-v-black--10355347 -> united-states-v-black--821235 (consolidated-repair session)\", \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Black.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:51:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-brinkley--4805913\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Brinkley\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Brinkley\", \"cite\": \"980 F.3d 377 (2020)\", \"year\": 2020, \"court\": \"4th Cir. 2020\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 4th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4805913/united-states-v-kendrick-brinkley/\", \"holding\": \"To enter a home to execute an arrest warrant, officers must have reason to believe — which in the Fourth Circuit means probable cause — both that the home is the suspect's residence and that the suspect is present; an entry supported only by an arrest warrant and uncorroborated hunches about residence and presence is unlawful, so the evidence obtained must be suppressed.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"seizures/arrests/Arrest in the Home.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/arrests/Arrest in the Home.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Arrest in the Home\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/arrests/Arrest in the Home.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Brinkley\", \"cite\": \"980 F.3d 377 (2020)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"To enter a home to execute an arrest warrant, officers must have reason to believe — which in the Fourth Circuit means probable cause — both that the home is the suspect's residence and that the suspect is present; an entry supported only by an arrest warrant and uncorroborated hunches about residence and presence is unlawful, so the evidence obtained must be suppressed.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Brinkley.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:52:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-camou--2759861\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Camou\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Camou\", \"cite\": \"773 F.3d 932 (2014)\", \"year\": 2014, \"court\": \"9th Cir. 2014\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 9th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/2759861/united-states-v-chad-camou/\", \"holding\": \"A cell phone found in a vehicle is not a 'container' for purposes of the automobile (vehicle) exception, so probable cause to search the vehicle does not authorize a warrantless search of the phone's data; extending Riley v. California, the search of Camou's phone — untimely as a search incident to arrest, unsupported by exigency, and outside the vehicle exception — was unconstitutional and was not saved by inevitable discovery or good faith.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"warrant-exceptions/searching-a-vehicle/Automobile Exception.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/searching-a-vehicle/Automobile Exception.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Automobile Exception\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/searching-a-vehicle/Automobile Exception.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Camou\", \"cite\": \"773 F.3d 932 (2014)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"A cell phone found in a vehicle is not a 'container' for purposes of the automobile (vehicle) exception, so probable cause to search the vehicle does not authorize a warrantless search of the phone's data; extending Riley v.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Camou.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:53:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-carlton-williams--4522771\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Carlton Williams\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Carlton Williams\", \"cite\": \"898 F.3d 323 (2018)\", \"year\": 2018, \"court\": \"3d Cir. 2018\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 3d Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4522771/united-states-v-carlton-williams/\", \"holding\": \"A suspect who has voluntarily consented to a search may withdraw that consent, but only by an unambiguous act or unequivocal statement that an objective viewer would understand as a desire to stop the search; ambiguous complaints or impatience do not withdraw consent, so evidence found before any clear withdrawal remains admissible.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"warrant-exceptions/Consent Searches.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/Consent Searches.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Consent Searches\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/Consent Searches.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Carlton Williams\", \"cite\": \"898 F.3d 323 (2018)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"A suspect who has voluntarily consented to a search may withdraw that consent, but only by an unambiguous act or unequivocal statement that an objective viewer would understand as a desire to stop the search; ambiguous complaints or impatience do not withdraw consent, so evidence found before any clear withdrawal remains admissible.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Carlton Williams.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:54:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-daniels--9500360\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Daniels\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Daniels\", \"cite\": \"101 F.4th 770 (2024)\", \"year\": 2024, \"court\": \"10th Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 10th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/9500360/united-states-v-daniels/\", \"holding\": \"The totality of the circumstances did not establish reasonable suspicion to detain Daniels: a near-anonymous, non-emergency tip that alleged no illegality and described men in dark clothing — which Daniels, in a bright orange jumpsuit, did not match — plus his mere proximity to the described SUV in a high-crime area late at night amounted only to an arbitrary hunch, so suppressing his name as the fruit of the unlawful detention was proper.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"seizures/Terry Stops and Reasonable Suspicion.md\", \"standards-of-proof/Probable Cause and Reasonable Suspicion.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/Terry Stops and Reasonable Suspicion.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}, {\"home\": \"standards-of-proof/Probable Cause and Reasonable Suspicion.md\", \"role\": \"Related (cross-doctrine)\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Terry Stops and Reasonable Suspicion\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/Terry Stops and Reasonable Suspicion.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Daniels\", \"cite\": \"101 F.4th 770 (2024)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"The totality of the circumstances did not establish reasonable suspicion to detain Daniels: a near-anonymous, non-emergency tip that alleged no illegality and described men in dark clothing — which Daniels, in a bright orange jumpsuit, did not match — plus his mere proximity to the described SUV in a high-crime area late at night amounted only to an arbitrary hunch, so suppressing his name as the fruit of the unlawful detention was proper.\"}, {\"home\": \"standards-of-proof/Probable Cause and Reasonable Suspicion.md\", \"role\": \"Related (cross-doctrine)\", \"role_class\": \"related\", \"schema\": \"related\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Daniels\", \"cite\": \"101 F.4th 770 (2024)\", \"relevance_tag_hint\": null, \"relevance_cell_hint\": null, \"primary_home\": \"Terry Stops and Reasonable Suspicion\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Daniels.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:55:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-ganias--3207604\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Ganias\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Ganias\", \"cite\": \"824 F.3d 199 (2016)\", \"year\": 2016, \"court\": \"2d Cir. en banc\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 2d Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/3207604/united-states-v-ganias/\", \"holding\": \"Sitting en banc, the Second Circuit affirmed Ganias's tax-evasion conviction on good-faith grounds — the agents' reliance on the 2006 warrant to search forensic mirror images retained from a 2003 search was objectively reasonable under Leon — and therefore expressly declined to decide whether the Government's years-long retention of non-responsive mirrored computer data beyond the 2003 warrant's scope violated the Fourth Amendment, displacing the panel's contrary holding.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"searches/Plain View Doctrine.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"searches/Plain View Doctrine.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Plain View Doctrine\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"searches/Plain View Doctrine.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Ganias\", \"cite\": \"824 F.3d 199 (2016)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"En banc, the Second Circuit affirmed on *Leon* good faith and **expressly left open** whether retaining non-responsive computer-mirror data beyond a warrant's scope violates the Fourth Amendment — displacing the panel's over-retention holding.\"}], \"worklist_note\": \"2026-07-07 orchestrator: spurious Border Searches home REMOVED per pre-W5 audit (Ganias = 2d Cir. data-retention/over-seizure case, no border nexus); Plain View home stands.\", \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Ganias.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:56:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-hanapel--10038262\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Hanapel\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Hanapel\", \"cite\": \"112 F.4th 539 (2024)\", \"year\": 2024, \"court\": \"8th Cir. 2024\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 8th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/10038262/united-states-v-james-hanapel/\", \"holding\": \"The Eighth Circuit affirmed the denial of judgment of acquittal, holding that Hanapel failed to establish entrapment as a matter of law: there was no government inducement as a matter of law, and his initial hesitation on learning the decoy's age did not negate the predisposition a reasonable jury could find from his ready pursuit of the opportunity — arriving at the meeting place with condoms within hours.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"b\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"fair-trial-and-reliability-doctrines/Entrapment.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"fair-trial-and-reliability-doctrines/Entrapment.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Entrapment\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"fair-trial-and-reliability-doctrines/Entrapment.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Hanapel\", \"cite\": \"112 F.4th 539 (2024)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"**Predisposition controls:** a defendant's initial reluctance on learning the decoy's age does not establish lack of predisposition as a matter of law where he then readily pursues the opportunity; no inducement as a matter of law from an undercover sting.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Hanapel.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:57:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-hay--9485331\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Hay\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Hay\", \"cite\": \"95 F.4th 1304 (2024)\", \"year\": 2024, \"court\": \"10th Cir. 2024\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 10th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/9485331/united-states-v-hay/\", \"holding\": \"The Tenth Circuit affirmed, holding that a fixed pole camera trained on the exterior of Hay's home — recording roughly fifteen hours a day for sixty-eight days but capturing only what was visible to passersby in public view — was not a Fourth Amendment search under the circuit's Jackson rule, and that Carpenter's mosaic theory of the 'whole of physical movements' does not disturb that rule for conventional, single-location camera surveillance of a home's exterior.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"searches/the-third-party-doctrine-and-digital-surveillance/index.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"searches/the-third-party-doctrine-and-digital-surveillance/index.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"The Third Party Doctrine And Digital Surveillance\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"searches/the-third-party-doctrine-and-digital-surveillance/index.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Hay\", \"cite\": \"95 F.4th 1304 (2024)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"A fixed pole camera recording only what is visible to passersby — even ~15 hours a day for 68 days — is not a Fourth Amendment search; *Carpenter*'s mosaic theory does not disturb the circuit's *Jackson* rule for conventional camera surveillance of a home's exterior.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Hay.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:58:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-hunt--10661637\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Hunt\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Hunt\", \"cite\": \"No. 23-2342, slip op. (9th Cir. 2025)\", \"year\": 2025, \"court\": \"9th Cir. 2025\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 9th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/10661637/united-states-v-hunt/\", \"holding\": \"The Ninth Circuit held that the abandonment doctrine applies to digital devices but that a court must analyze the intent to abandon the physical device separately from the intent to abandon its data; Hunt did not abandon his iPhone or its contents by dropping it after being shot five times and fleeing for medical help, so the district court erred in finding he lacked standing — but his Fourth Amendment claim nonetheless failed on the merits because agents obtained a warrant and searched the phone within a reasonable period; conviction affirmed.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"searches/Abandonment.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"searches/Abandonment.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Abandonment\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"searches/Abandonment.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Hunt\", \"cite\": \"No. 23-2342, slip op. (9th Cir. 2025)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"Abandonment applies to phones, but courts must weigh the intent to abandon the **device** separately from the intent to abandon its **data**; dropping an iPhone after being shot and fleeing for medical help is not abandonment of the data.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Hunt.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:59:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-kolsuz--4499413\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Kolsuz\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Kolsuz\", \"cite\": \"890 F.3d 133 (2018)\", \"year\": 2018, \"court\": \"4th Cir. 2018\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 4th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4499413/united-states-v-hamza-kolsuz/\", \"holding\": \"The Fourth Circuit held that a month-long off-site forensic examination of a smartphone seized at an international airport is a border search, but that under Riley it is a nonroutine border search requiring at least individualized suspicion — while expressly reserving whether reasonable suspicion suffices or a probable-cause warrant is required — and affirmed the denial of suppression under the good-faith exception because the agents reasonably relied on precedent holding that no warrant was required.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"warrant-exceptions/programmatic-and-special-needs-searches/Border Searches.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/programmatic-and-special-needs-searches/Border Searches.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Border Searches\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/programmatic-and-special-needs-searches/Border Searches.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Kolsuz\", \"cite\": \"890 F.3d 133 (2018)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"A forensic (as opposed to manual) search of a smartphone at the border is a **nonroutine** border search requiring at least individualized suspicion under *Riley*; the court reserved reasonable-suspicion-vs-warrant and affirmed on good faith.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Kolsuz.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:61:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-liddell--1461978\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Liddell\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Liddell\", \"cite\": \"517 F.3d 1007 (2008)\", \"year\": 2008, \"court\": \"8th Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 8th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/1461978/united-states-v-liddell/\", \"holding\": \"The Eighth Circuit held that an un-Mirandized, in-custody question to a secured arrestee — 'Is there anything else in there we need to know about?' after officers found a concealed revolver in his car — fell within New York v. Quarles's public-safety exception to Miranda, because the risk that officers might mishandle other unknown weapons while searching a vehicle incident to a late-night arrest is an objectively reasonable public-safety concern; the incriminating statement was admissible and the felon-in-possession conviction affirmed.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"confessions-interrogation-and-the-fifth-amendment/Miranda and Custodial Interrogation.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"confessions-interrogation-and-the-fifth-amendment/Miranda and Custodial Interrogation.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Miranda and Custodial Interrogation\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"confessions-interrogation-and-the-fifth-amendment/Miranda and Custodial Interrogation.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Liddell\", \"cite\": \"517 F.3d 1007 (2008)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"The *Quarles* public-safety exception to *Miranda* reaches a post-arrest question to a **secured** suspect about other weapons or contraband in a space about to be searched — the risk of officers mishandling unknown weapons is an objectively reasonable public-safety concern.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Liddell.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:62:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-loera--4619076\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Loera\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Loera\", \"cite\": \"923 F.3d 907 (2019)\", \"year\": 2019, \"court\": \"10th Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 10th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4619076/united-states-v-loera/\", \"holding\": \"The Tenth Circuit held that the Fourth Amendment does not require officers executing an electronic-search warrant to stop when they discover evidence of a different, out-of-scope crime, so long as their search remains directed at uncovering the evidence the warrant specifies; agents who found child pornography while searching Loera's devices for computer-fraud evidence could continue the authorized search, and — having obtained a second, pornography-specific warrant to search for that evidence — the denial of suppression was affirmed.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"searches/Plain View Doctrine.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"searches/Plain View Doctrine.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Plain View Doctrine\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"searches/Plain View Doctrine.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Loera\", \"cite\": \"923 F.3d 907 (2019)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"Officers executing an electronic-search warrant need not stop when they stumble on evidence of a different crime, so long as the search stays directed at the warrant's specified evidence — but a search *for* the out-of-scope evidence needs its own warrant.\"}], \"worklist_note\": \"2026-07-07 orchestrator remap-2 post-re-key: united-states-v-loera--10386176 -> united-states-v-loera--4619076 (pre-W5 audit session)\", \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Loera.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:63:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-loines--9357039\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Loines\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Loines\", \"cite\": \"56 F.4th 1099 (2023)\", \"year\": 2023, \"court\": \"6th Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 6th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/9357039/united-states-v-aaron-loines/\", \"holding\": \"The Sixth Circuit reversed the denial of suppression and vacated the conviction, holding that the plain-view doctrine did not supply probable cause to search Loines's car: a detective's claim to have seen a 'bag of dope' through a tinted window was not plausible on the record, and in any event the objects — a plastic bag near a cigar wrapper and a lottery ticket — were not immediately and apparently incriminating from outside the car, their criminal character emerging only after officers entered and closely inspected the console, itself a further search unsupported by probable cause.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"searches/Plain View Doctrine.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"searches/Plain View Doctrine.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Plain View Doctrine\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"searches/Plain View Doctrine.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Loines\", \"cite\": \"56 F.4th 1099 (2023)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"Plain view fails on the **immediately-apparent** prong: a plastic bag near a cigar wrapper and lottery ticket, whose criminal character appeared only after officers entered and closely inspected the console, was not immediately incriminating — no probable cause, and the close inspection was itself a further search.\"}], \"worklist_note\": \"2026-07-07 orchestrator remap-2 post-re-key: united-states-v-loines--9357144 -> united-states-v-loines--9357039 (pre-W5 audit session)\", \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Loines.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:64:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-lyle--8443943\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Lyle\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Lyle\", \"cite\": \"919 F.3d 716 (2019)\", \"year\": 2019, \"court\": \"2d Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 2d Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/8443943/united-states-v-lyle/\", \"holding\": \"On remand from the Supreme Court in light of Byrd v. United States, the Second Circuit reaffirmed that Lyle lacked standing to challenge the inventory search of a rental car, holding that he had no reasonable expectation of privacy in it because he was not merely an unauthorized driver but an unlicensed one — his possession was both unauthorized and unlawful — so Byrd, which protects an unauthorized driver in lawful possession, did not require a different result; convictions affirmed.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"the-exclusionary-rule-remedies-and-standing/Standing to Challenge a Search.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"the-exclusionary-rule-remedies-and-standing/Standing to Challenge a Search.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Standing to Challenge a Search\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"the-exclusionary-rule-remedies-and-standing/Standing to Challenge a Search.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Lyle\", \"cite\": \"919 F.3d 716 (2019)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"Post-*Byrd* standing: an unauthorized rental-car driver who is also **unlicensed** has no reasonable expectation of privacy — his possession is unauthorized *and* unlawful, so *Byrd*'s protection for a driver in lawful possession does not reach him.\"}], \"worklist_note\": \"2026-07-07 orchestrator remap-2 post-re-key: united-states-v-lyle--8435375 -> united-states-v-lyle--8443943 (pre-W5 audit session)\", \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Lyle.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:65:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-maez--521939\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Maez\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Maez\", \"cite\": \"872 F.2d 1444 (1989)\", \"year\": 1989, \"court\": \"U.S. Court of Appeals, 10th Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 10th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/521939/united-states-v-arthur-maez/\", \"holding\": \"The Tenth Circuit held that police effected an unlawful warrantless arrest in the home in violation of Payton v. New York when, without an arrest warrant, a SWAT team surrounded Maez's mobile home and ordered the occupants out over loudspeakers, coercing him from the home into custody — physical entry across the threshold is not required, because such a show of force that makes a suspect come out under coercion is a Payton violation — so the evidence obtained after the tainted arrest required suppression, and the court reversed.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"seizures/arrests/Arrest in the Home.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/arrests/Arrest in the Home.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Arrest in the Home\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/arrests/Arrest in the Home.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Maez\", \"cite\": \"872 F.2d 1444 (1989)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"A *Payton* violation does not require crossing the threshold: a warrantless SWAT-team show of force that surrounds a home and orders the suspect out — coercing him from the home into custody — is an unlawful warrantless arrest in the home.\"}], \"worklist_note\": \"2026-07-07 orchestrator remap post-re-key: united-states-v-maez--7355106 -> united-states-v-maez--521939 (consolidated-repair session)\", \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Maez.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:66:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-massenburg--223188\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Massenburg\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Massenburg\", \"cite\": \"654 F.3d 480 (2011)\", \"year\": 2011, \"court\": \"4th Cir. 2011\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 4th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/223188/united-states-v-massenburg/\", \"holding\": \"The Fourth Circuit reversed the denial of suppression, holding that the nonconsensual frisk of Massenburg was not supported by reasonable suspicion and — critically for the collective-knowledge doctrine — that Officer Fries's uncommunicated observation of a 'bulge' could not be imputed to the frisking officer: the collective-knowledge (fellow-officer) doctrine substitutes an instructing officer's knowledge for the acting officer's only where the information was communicated, and does not permit after-the-fact aggregation of uncommunicated facts among officers.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"seizures/Collective Knowledge and the Fellow-Officer Rule.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/Collective Knowledge and the Fellow-Officer Rule.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Collective Knowledge and the Fellow-Officer Rule\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/Collective Knowledge and the Fellow-Officer Rule.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Massenburg\", \"cite\": \"654 F.3d 480 (2011)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"The collective-knowledge / fellow-officer doctrine is **vertical** only: it substitutes an *instructing* officer's knowledge for the acting officer's where the information was **communicated** — it does not allow after-the-fact 'horizontal' aggregation of uncommunicated facts, so one officer's unshared observation of a bulge cannot supply reasonable suspicion for another's frisk.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Massenburg.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:67:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-may-shaw--4743325\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. May-Shaw\", \"caption\": \"United States v. May-Shaw\", \"cite\": \"955 F.3d 563 (2020)\", \"year\": 2020, \"court\": \"6th Cir. 2020\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 6th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4743325/united-states-v-christopher-may-shaw/\", \"holding\": \"The Sixth Circuit affirmed, holding that a covered carport in a communal apartment parking lot — where May-Shaw regularly parked but had no right to exclude others, and which was easily viewable from a public street — was not within the curtilage of his apartment under the Dunn factors, so a drug-dog sniff of his car parked there was not a Fourth Amendment search; nor did the twenty-three-day pole-camera surveillance of the lot violate any reasonable expectation of privacy.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"searches/Curtilage.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"searches/Curtilage.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Curtilage\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"searches/Curtilage.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. May-Shaw\", \"cite\": \"955 F.3d 563 (2020)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"Applying the *Dunn* factors, a communal covered carport that the defendant has no right to exclude others from — and that is easily viewable from a public street — is **not** within the curtilage of his apartment, so a dog sniff of a car parked there is not a search.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. May-Shaw.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:68:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-mayville--4742862\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Mayville\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Mayville\", \"cite\": \"955 F.3d 825 (2020)\", \"year\": 2020, \"court\": \"10th Cir. 2020\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 10th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4742862/united-states-v-mayville/\", \"holding\": \"The Tenth Circuit affirmed, holding that a nineteen-minute traffic stop that ended with a drug-dog alert did not violate Rodriguez v. United States: an officer may run a criminal-history (Triple I) check through dispatch as a negligibly burdensome safety precaution, and because the troopers diligently pursued the stop's mission and the dog sniff was contemporaneous with that pursuit — the alert coming just before the records check returned — the stop was not unreasonably prolonged, since reasonableness, not efficiency, is the touchstone of the Fourth Amendment.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"seizures/Traffic Stops.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/Traffic Stops.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Traffic Stops\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/Traffic Stops.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Mayville\", \"cite\": \"955 F.3d 825 (2020)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"*Rodriguez* bars prolonging a stop beyond its mission, but reasonableness — not efficiency — is the touchstone: a dog sniff contemporaneous with officers' diligent pursuit of the stop's tasks (records/criminal-history checks) does not unlawfully extend a ~19-minute stop.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Mayville.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:69:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-mendez--9524074\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Mendez\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Mendez\", \"cite\": \"103 F.4th 1303 (2024)\", \"year\": 2024, \"court\": \"7th Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 7th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/9524074/united-states-v-marcos-mendez/\", \"holding\": \"The Seventh Circuit affirmed, joining its sister circuits to hold that a border search of a cell phone or other electronic device requires neither a warrant nor probable cause, and that a brief, manual search of a traveler's phone at the border (here, scrolling the photo gallery at O'Hare) is a routine border search requiring no individualized suspicion; because that valid manual search revealed child pornography, the court did not need to decide whether the later forensic extraction required reasonable suspicion, since the agents had ample suspicion by then.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"warrant-exceptions/programmatic-and-special-needs-searches/Border Searches.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/programmatic-and-special-needs-searches/Border Searches.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Border Searches\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/programmatic-and-special-needs-searches/Border Searches.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Mendez\", \"cite\": \"103 F.4th 1303 (2024)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"Joining the cross-circuit consensus, a **brief, manual** search of a traveler's phone at the border (an international airport) is a **routine** border search requiring no individualized suspicion, and no border device search requires a warrant or probable cause; the forensic-search standard was left open.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Mendez.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:71:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-reddick--4527853\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Reddick\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Reddick\", \"cite\": \"900 F.3d 636 (2018)\", \"year\": 2018, \"court\": \"5th Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 5th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4527853/united-states-v-henry-reddick/\", \"holding\": \"Under the private-search doctrine, the government does not conduct a Fourth Amendment search when it merely receives and reviews the results of a search already performed by a private party, so where a private company hash-matched Reddick's uploaded files to known child-pornography images and reported them, the officer's warrantless viewing of the flagged images exposed nothing beyond what the private search had already revealed and did not violate the Fourth Amendment.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"foundations-and-the-fourth-amendment/Fourth Amendment Framework.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"foundations-and-the-fourth-amendment/Fourth Amendment Framework.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Fourth Amendment Framework\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"foundations-and-the-fourth-amendment/Fourth Amendment Framework.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Reddick\", \"cite\": \"900 F.3d 636 (2018)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"Under the private-search doctrine, the government does not conduct a Fourth Amendment search when it merely receives and reviews the results of a search already performed by a private party, so where a private company hash-matched Reddick's uploaded files to known child-pornography images and reported them, the officer's warrantless viewing of the flagged images exposed nothing beyond what the private search had already revealed and did not violate the Fourth Amendment.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Reddick.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:72:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-small--4684957\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Small\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Small\", \"cite\": \"944 F.3d 490 (2019)\", \"year\": 2019, \"court\": \"4th Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 4th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4684957/united-states-v-dontae-small/\", \"holding\": \"A person who intentionally discards property to evade capture abandons any reasonable expectation of privacy in it, and abandonment is assessed on the objective information available to officers at the time of the search, so where Small crashed through a security gate, fled on foot, and threw down his cell phone along with other belongings, the warrantless searches of the phone did not violate the Fourth Amendment and Riley did not preserve the phone's digital contents once the physical device was abandoned.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"searches/Abandonment.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"searches/Abandonment.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Abandonment\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"searches/Abandonment.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Small\", \"cite\": \"944 F.3d 490 (2019)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"A person who intentionally discards property to evade capture abandons any reasonable expectation of privacy in it, and abandonment is assessed on the objective information available to officers at the time of the search, so where Small crashed through a security gate, fled on foot, and threw down his cell phone along with other belongings, the warrantless searches of the phone did not violate the Fourth Amendment and Riley did not preserve the phone's digital contents once the physical device was abandoned.\"}], \"worklist_note\": \"2026-07-07 orchestrator remap-2 post-re-key: united-states-v-small--10593041 -> united-states-v-small--4684957 (pre-W5 audit session)\", \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Small.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:73:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-oliveras--9484364\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Oliveras\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Oliveras\", \"cite\": \"96 F.4th 298 (2024)\", \"year\": 2024, \"court\": \"2d Cir. 2024\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 2d Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/9484364/united-states-v-oliveras/\", \"holding\": \"Under the special-needs doctrine, a district court may impose a special condition of supervised release authorizing a probation officer to conduct suspicionless searches of the defendant's person, property, vehicle, or residence, where the record sufficiently supports it under 18 U.S.C. § 3583(d); but because the district court here made no individualized assessment tying the search condition to the statutory factors, the condition was vacated and remanded even though the special-needs authorization itself was sound.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"warrant-exceptions/programmatic-and-special-needs-searches/Special Needs and Administrative Searches.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/programmatic-and-special-needs-searches/Special Needs and Administrative Searches.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Special Needs and Administrative Searches\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/programmatic-and-special-needs-searches/Special Needs and Administrative Searches.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Oliveras\", \"cite\": \"96 F.4th 298 (2024)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"Under the special-needs doctrine, a district court may impose a special condition of supervised release authorizing a probation officer to conduct suspicionless searches of the defendant's person, property, vehicle, or residence, where the record sufficiently supports it under 18 U.S.C.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Oliveras.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:74:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-meyer--5302394\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Meyer\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Meyer\", \"cite\": \"19 F.4th 1028 (2021)\", \"year\": 2021, \"court\": \"8th Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 8th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/5302394/united-states-v-william-meyer/\", \"holding\": \"A 'knock and talk' — approaching a home and knocking to ask questions — is a valid investigative technique, and where a suspect's evasive answers during such an encounter give officers an objectively reasonable basis to believe he will destroy digital evidence if left alone, the resulting exigency justifies a warrantless entry and seizure, provided the officers did not manufacture the exigency by threatening to violate the Fourth Amendment.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"warrant-exceptions/Knock and Talk.md\", \"warrant-exceptions/home-entry-and-search/Exigent Circumstances and Hot Pursuit.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/Knock and Talk.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}, {\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/home-entry-and-search/Exigent Circumstances and Hot Pursuit.md\", \"role\": \"Related (cross-doctrine)\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Knock and Talk\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/Knock and Talk.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Meyer\", \"cite\": \"19 F.4th 1028 (2021)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"A 'knock and talk' — approaching a home and knocking to ask questions — is a valid investigative technique, and where a suspect's evasive answers during such an encounter give officers an objectively reasonable basis to believe he will destroy digital evidence if left alone, the resulting exigency justifies a warrantless entry and seizure, provided the officers did not manufacture the exigency by threatening to violate the Fourth Amendment.\"}, {\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/home-entry-and-search/Exigent Circumstances and Hot Pursuit.md\", \"role\": \"Related (cross-doctrine)\", \"role_class\": \"related\", \"schema\": \"related\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Meyer\", \"cite\": \"19 F.4th 1028 (2021)\", \"relevance_tag_hint\": null, \"relevance_cell_hint\": null, \"primary_home\": \"Knock and Talk\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Meyer.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:75:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-xiang--9397097\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Xiang\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Xiang\", \"cite\": \"67 F.4th 895 (2023)\", \"year\": 2023, \"court\": \"8th Cir. 2023\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 8th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/9397097/united-states-v-haitao-xiang/\", \"holding\": \"The border-search exception applies with equal force to travelers and objects leaving the country, so CBP's warrantless seizure and forensic examination of Haitao Xiang's electronic devices as he departed for China fell within the exception; the court adopted the consensus that a non-routine forensic device search requires reasonable, individualized suspicion but not a warrant or probable cause, and held the officers had reasonable suspicion here, so it affirmed the denial of suppression.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"warrant-exceptions/programmatic-and-special-needs-searches/Border Searches.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/programmatic-and-special-needs-searches/Border Searches.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Border Searches\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/programmatic-and-special-needs-searches/Border Searches.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Xiang\", \"cite\": \"67 F.4th 895 (2023)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"The border-search exception applies with equal force to travelers and objects leaving the country, so CBP's warrantless seizure and forensic examination of Haitao Xiang's electronic devices as he departed for China fell within the exception; the court adopted the consensus that a non-routine forensic device search requires reasonable, individualized suspicion but not a warrant or probable cause, and held the officers had reasonable suspicion here, so it affirmed the denial of suppression.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Xiang.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:76:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-payne--9494371\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Payne\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Payne\", \"cite\": \"99 F.4th 495 (2024)\", \"year\": 2024, \"court\": \"9th Cir. 2024\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 9th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/9494371/united-states-v-jeremy-payne/\", \"holding\": \"A California parolee is subject to a valid suspicionless search condition, and because parole searches require no probable cause as to the place or thing searched, CHP officers who stopped Payne for a traffic violation and, learning he was a parolee, searched his cell phone acted reasonably; the court declined to extend Riley — a search-incident-to-arrest case — to parole searches of a cell phone, and separately held that compelling Payne to unlock the phone with his thumbprint did not violate the Fifth Amendment because the act was non-testimonial.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"warrant-exceptions/programmatic-and-special-needs-searches/Special Needs and Administrative Searches.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/programmatic-and-special-needs-searches/Special Needs and Administrative Searches.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Special Needs and Administrative Searches\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/programmatic-and-special-needs-searches/Special Needs and Administrative Searches.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Payne\", \"cite\": \"99 F.4th 495 (2024)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"A California parolee is subject to a valid suspicionless search condition, and because parole searches require no probable cause as to the place or thing searched, CHP officers who stopped Payne for a traffic violation and, learning he was a parolee, searched his cell phone acted reasonably; the court declined to extend Riley — a search-incident-to-arrest case — to parole searches of a cell phone, and separately held that compelling Payne to unlock the phone with his thumbprint did not violate the Fifth Amendment because the act was non-testimonial.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Payne.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:77:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-young--4766220\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Young\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Young\", \"cite\": \"964 F.3d 938 (2020)\", \"year\": 2020, \"court\": \"U.S. Court of Appeals, 10th Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 10th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4766220/united-states-v-young/\", \"holding\": \"A confession is involuntary under the Due Process Clause when, under the totality of the circumstances, the defendant's capacity for self-determination is critically impaired by coercive police conduct; where an agent materially misrepresented the sentence the defendant faced, falsely promised to speak to a federal judge about his cooperation, and dangled leniency, and the defendant's ordinary personal characteristics could not withstand that pressure, the resulting confession was involuntary and had to be suppressed.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"confessions-interrogation-and-the-fifth-amendment/Due-Process Voluntariness of Confessions.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"confessions-interrogation-and-the-fifth-amendment/Due-Process Voluntariness of Confessions.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Due-Process Voluntariness of Confessions\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"confessions-interrogation-and-the-fifth-amendment/Due-Process Voluntariness of Confessions.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Young\", \"cite\": \"964 F.3d 938 (2020)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"A confession is involuntary under the Due Process Clause when, under the totality of the circumstances, the defendant's capacity for self-determination is critically impaired by coercive police conduct; where an agent materially misrepresented the sentence the defendant faced, falsely promised to speak to a federal judge about his cooperation, and dangled leniency, and the defendant's ordinary personal characteristics could not withstand that pressure, the resulting confession was involuntary and had to be suppressed.\"}], \"worklist_note\": \"2026-07-07 orchestrator remap post-re-key: united-states-v-young--10687648 -> united-states-v-young--4766220 (consolidated-repair session)\", \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Young.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:78:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-perez--9456060\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Perez\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Perez\", \"cite\": \"89 F.4th 247 (2023)\", \"year\": 2023, \"court\": \"1st Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 1st Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/9456060/united-states-v-perez/\", \"holding\": \"The search-incident-to-arrest exception permits a warrantless search of an arrestee's nearby container, and the First Circuit's decision in United States v. Eatherton — upholding such a search of a bag within an arrestee's reach — retains controlling force notwithstanding the Supreme Court's intervening decisions in Gant and Riley, so the warrantless search of Perez's backpack incident to his arrest did not violate the Fourth Amendment and his conviction was affirmed.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"warrant-exceptions/Search Incident to Arrest.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/Search Incident to Arrest.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Search Incident to Arrest\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/Search Incident to Arrest.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Perez\", \"cite\": \"89 F.4th 247 (2023)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"The search-incident-to-arrest exception permits a warrantless search of an arrestee's nearby container, and the First Circuit's decision in United States v.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Perez.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:79:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-perez-rodriguez--5067201\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Perez-Rodriguez\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Perez-Rodriguez\", \"cite\": \"13 F.4th 1 (2021)\", \"year\": 2021, \"court\": \"1st Cir. 2021\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 1st Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/5067201/united-states-v-perez-rodriguez/\", \"holding\": \"The entrapment defense has two prongs — improper government inducement and the defendant's lack of predisposition — and a defendant who makes a modest production showing on both is entitled to have the jury instructed on entrapment; where an undercover agent posing on a dating app steered a target toward a sexual encounter with a fictitious minor, the district court's refusal to give the entrapment instruction was plain error, and the conviction was vacated and remanded for a new trial.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"b\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"fair-trial-and-reliability-doctrines/Entrapment.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"fair-trial-and-reliability-doctrines/Entrapment.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Entrapment\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"fair-trial-and-reliability-doctrines/Entrapment.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Perez-Rodriguez\", \"cite\": \"13 F.4th 1 (2021)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"The entrapment defense has two prongs — improper government inducement and the defendant's lack of predisposition — and a defendant who makes a modest production showing on both is entitled to have the jury instructed on entrapment; where an undercover agent posing on a dating app steered a target toward a sexual encounter with a fictitious minor, the district court's refusal to give the entrapment instruction was plain error, and the conviction was vacated and remanded for a new trial.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Perez-Rodriguez.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:80:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-wilson--5296785\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Wilson\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Wilson\", \"cite\": \"13 F.4th 961 (2021)\", \"year\": 2021, \"court\": \"9th Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 9th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/5296785/united-states-v-luke-wilson/\", \"holding\": \"Under the private-search doctrine of Walter and Jacobsen, the government may repeat a private party's search without a warrant only insofar as it does not exceed the scope of that private search; where Google's automated system flagged four email attachments as matching known child-pornography hashes but no person had actually viewed those images, a government agent's warrantless opening and viewing of them exceeded the antecedent private search — learning new information and expanding the intrusion — so it was not justified by the private-search exception, and Wilson's conviction was reversed.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"foundations-and-the-fourth-amendment/Fourth Amendment Framework.md\", \"searches/two-definitions-of-search/index.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"foundations-and-the-fourth-amendment/Fourth Amendment Framework.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}, {\"home\": \"searches/two-definitions-of-search/index.md\", \"role\": \"Related (cross-doctrine)\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Fourth Amendment Framework\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"foundations-and-the-fourth-amendment/Fourth Amendment Framework.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Wilson\", \"cite\": \"13 F.4th 961 (2021)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"Under the private-search doctrine of Walter and Jacobsen, the government may repeat a private party's search without a warrant only insofar as it does not exceed the scope of that private search; where Google's automated system flagged four email attachments as matching known child-pornography hashes but no person had actually viewed those images, a government agent's warrantless opening and viewing of them exceeded the antecedent private search — learning new information and expanding the intrusion — so it was not justified by the private-search exception, and Wilson's conviction was reversed.\"}, {\"home\": \"searches/two-definitions-of-search/index.md\", \"role\": \"Related (cross-doctrine)\", \"role_class\": \"related\", \"schema\": \"related\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Wilson\", \"cite\": \"13 F.4th 961 (2021)\", \"relevance_tag_hint\": null, \"relevance_cell_hint\": null, \"primary_home\": \"Fourth Amendment Framework\"}], \"worklist_note\": \"2026-07-07 orchestrator remap-2 post-re-key: united-states-v-wilson--10664712 -> united-states-v-wilson--5296785 (pre-W5 audit session)\", \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Wilson.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:81:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-ruckman--480405\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Ruckman\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Ruckman\", \"cite\": \"806 F.2d 1471 (1986)\", \"year\": 1986, \"court\": \"10th Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 10th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/480405/united-states-v-frank-william-ruckman/\", \"holding\": \"A person who lives without authorization in a natural cave on federal public land has no objectively reasonable expectation of privacy in it, because he can be ousted by the managing authorities at any time; the cave is not a Fourth Amendment 'house,' so the warrantless search that produced the charged contraband did not fall within the Fourth Amendment's protection, and the denial of suppression was affirmed.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"searches/Tents.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"searches/Tents.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Tents\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"searches/Tents.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Ruckman\", \"cite\": \"806 F.2d 1471 (1986)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"A person who lives without authorization in a natural cave on federal public land has no objectively reasonable expectation of privacy in it, because he can be ousted by the managing authorities at any time; the cave is not a Fourth Amendment 'house,' so the warrantless search that produced the charged contraband did not fall within the Fourth Amendment's protection, and the denial of suppression was affirmed.\"}], \"worklist_note\": \"2026-07-07 orchestrator remap-2 post-re-key: united-states-v-ruckman--8699562 -> united-states-v-ruckman--480405 (pre-W5 audit session)\", \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Ruckman.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:82:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-vasquez-algarin--3199633\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Vasquez-Algarin\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Vasquez-Algarin\", \"cite\": \"821 F.3d 467 (2016)\", \"year\": 2016, \"court\": \"3d Cir. 2016\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 3d Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/3199633/united-states-v-johnny-vasquez-algarin/\", \"holding\": \"To force entry into a dwelling to execute an arrest warrant, officers must have probable cause — not a lesser 'reasonable belief' — that the suspect both resides at and is present within the home; joining the Fifth, Sixth, Seventh, and Ninth Circuits, the Third Circuit held that Payton's 'reason to believe' language means probable cause, and because the officers here forced entry into a residence that was not shown to be the arrestee's home on that standard, the denial of suppression was reversed.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"seizures/arrests/Arrest in the Home.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/arrests/Arrest in the Home.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Arrest in the Home\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/arrests/Arrest in the Home.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Vasquez-Algarin\", \"cite\": \"821 F.3d 467 (2016)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"To force entry into a dwelling to execute an arrest warrant, officers must have probable cause — not a lesser 'reasonable belief' — that the suspect both resides at and is present within the home; joining the Fifth, Sixth, Seventh, and Ninth Circuits, the Third Circuit held that Payton's 'reason to believe' language means probable cause, and because the officers here forced entry into a residence that was not shown to be the arrestee's home on that standard, the denial of suppression was reversed.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Vasquez-Algarin.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:83:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-williams--793121\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Williams\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Williams\", \"cite\": \"435 F.3d 1148 (2006)\", \"year\": 2006, \"court\": \"U.S. Court of Appeals, 9th Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 9th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/793121/united-states-v-tashiri-wayne-williams/\", \"holding\": \"Under Missouri v. Seibert, a court must suppress a postwarning confession obtained through a deliberate two-step 'question-first' interrogation when the midstream Miranda warning was objectively ineffective at conveying to the suspect a genuine choice whether to follow up on his earlier, unwarned admission; because agents here questioned Williams until he confessed and only then gave the warning, and the district court — lacking the benefit of Seibert — never determined whether the two-step tactic was deliberate or the warning effective, the admission of the written confession was reversed.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"confessions-interrogation-and-the-fifth-amendment/Miranda Waiver and Invocation.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"confessions-interrogation-and-the-fifth-amendment/Miranda Waiver and Invocation.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Miranda Waiver and Invocation\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"confessions-interrogation-and-the-fifth-amendment/Miranda Waiver and Invocation.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Williams\", \"cite\": \"435 F.3d 1148 (2006)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"Under Missouri v.\"}], \"worklist_note\": \"2026-07-07 orchestrator remap post-re-key: united-states-v-williams--10670874 -> united-states-v-williams--793121 (consolidated-repair session)\", \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Williams.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:84:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-moore-bush--6476395\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Moore-Bush\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Moore-Bush\", \"cite\": \"36 F.4th 320 (2022)\", \"year\": 2022, \"court\": \"1st Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 1st Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/6476395/united-states-v-moore-bush/\", \"holding\": \"Sitting en banc, the First Circuit unanimously reversed the suppression of evidence from eight months of continuous, warrantless pole-camera surveillance of a home's front curtilage and remanded with instructions to deny suppression — but the court divided evenly on the merits: three judges concluded the prolonged aggregate surveillance was a Fourth Amendment search, and three concluded it was not, so the decision established no binding circuit rule on whether such surveillance is a search.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"foundations-and-the-fourth-amendment/Fourth Amendment Framework.md\", \"searches/Curtilage.md\", \"searches/the-third-party-doctrine-and-digital-surveillance/index.md\", \"searches/two-definitions-of-search/index.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"foundations-and-the-fourth-amendment/Fourth Amendment Framework.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}, {\"home\": \"searches/Curtilage.md\", \"role\": \"Related (cross-doctrine)\"}, {\"home\": \"searches/the-third-party-doctrine-and-digital-surveillance/index.md\", \"role\": \"Related (cross-doctrine)\"}, {\"home\": \"searches/two-definitions-of-search/index.md\", \"role\": \"Related (cross-doctrine)\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Fourth Amendment Framework\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"foundations-and-the-fourth-amendment/Fourth Amendment Framework.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Moore-Bush\", \"cite\": \"36 F.4th 320 (2022)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"Sitting en banc, the First Circuit unanimously reversed the suppression of evidence from eight months of continuous, warrantless pole-camera surveillance of a home's front curtilage and remanded with instructions to deny suppression — but the court divided evenly on the merits: three judges concluded the prolonged aggregate surveillance was a Fourth Amendment search, and three concluded it was not, so the decision established no binding circuit rule on whether such surveillance is a search.\"}, {\"home\": \"searches/Curtilage.md\", \"role\": \"Related (cross-doctrine)\", \"role_class\": \"related\", \"schema\": \"related\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Moore-Bush\", \"cite\": \"36 F.4th 320 (2022)\", \"relevance_tag_hint\": null, \"relevance_cell_hint\": null, \"primary_home\": \"Fourth Amendment Framework\"}, {\"home\": \"searches/the-third-party-doctrine-and-digital-surveillance/index.md\", \"role\": \"Related (cross-doctrine)\", \"role_class\": \"related\", \"schema\": \"related\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Moore-Bush\", \"cite\": \"36 F.4th 320 (2022)\", \"relevance_tag_hint\": null, \"relevance_cell_hint\": null, \"primary_home\": \"Fourth Amendment Framework\"}, {\"home\": \"searches/two-definitions-of-search/index.md\", \"role\": \"Related (cross-doctrine)\", \"role_class\": \"related\", \"schema\": \"related\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Moore-Bush\", \"cite\": \"36 F.4th 320 (2022)\", \"relevance_tag_hint\": null, \"relevance_cell_hint\": null, \"primary_home\": \"Fourth Amendment Framework\"}], \"worklist_note\": \"2026-07-07 orchestrator remap-2 post-re-key: united-states-v-moore-bush--6476396 -> united-states-v-moore-bush--6476395 (pre-W5 audit session)\", \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Moore-Bush.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:103:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"northrup-v-city-of-toledo-police-dept--2800431\", \"record_id_after\": \"Northrup v. City of Toledo Police Dept\", \"caption\": \"Northrup v. City of Toledo Police Dept\", \"cite\": \"785 F.3d 1128 (2015)\", \"year\": 2015, \"court\": \"U.S. Court of Appeals, 6th Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 6th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/2800431/shawn-northrup-v-city-of-toledo-police-dept/\", \"holding\": \"Where state law permits the open carry of firearms, the mere fact that a person is openly and lawfully carrying a holstered handgun — reported by a 911 caller — does not, without more, give an officer reasonable suspicion of criminality or dangerousness to justify stopping, disarming, and detaining him; doing so violates clearly established Fourth Amendment law.\", \"leg\": \"frontier\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"frontier-split\", \"homes\": [\"seizures/Terry Stops and Reasonable Suspicion.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/Terry Stops and Reasonable Suspicion.md\", \"role\": \"Illustrates a circuit split\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Terry Stops and Reasonable Suspicion\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/Terry Stops and Reasonable Suspicion.md\", \"role\": \"Illustrates a circuit split\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"Northrup v. City of Toledo Police Dept\", \"cite\": \"785 F.3d 1128 (2015)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"Where state law permits the open carry of firearms, the mere fact that a person is openly and lawfully carrying a holstered handgun — reported by a 911 caller — does not, without more, give an officer reasonable suspicion of criminality or dangerousness to justify stopping, disarming, and detaining him; doing so violates clearly established Fourth Amendment law.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/Northrup v. City of Toledo Police Dept.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:119:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-satterfield--8934150\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Satterfield\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Satterfield\", \"cite\": \"743 F.2d 827 (1984)\", \"year\": 1984, \"court\": \"U.S. Court of Appeals, 11th Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 11th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/8934150/united-states-v-satterfield/\", \"holding\": \"For the inevitable-discovery exception to the exclusionary rule to admit illegally seized evidence, the Eleventh Circuit requires not only a reasonable probability that the evidence would have been found by lawful means, but also that the lawful means were possessed by the police and were being actively pursued before the illegal conduct occurred; because officers obtained the search warrant only hours after the warrantless search, the shotgun was not admissible under inevitable discovery — though its erroneous admission was harmless in light of the overwhelming other evidence.\", \"leg\": \"frontier\", \"prong\": \"b\", \"basis\": \"frontier-split\", \"homes\": [\"the-exclusionary-rule-remedies-and-standing/the-exclusionary-rule/index.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"the-exclusionary-rule-remedies-and-standing/the-exclusionary-rule/index.md\", \"role\": \"Illustrates a circuit split\"}], \"primary_home\": \"The Exclusionary Rule\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"the-exclusionary-rule-remedies-and-standing/the-exclusionary-rule/index.md\", \"role\": \"Illustrates a circuit split\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Satterfield\", \"cite\": \"743 F.2d 827 (1984)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"For the inevitable-discovery exception to the exclusionary rule to admit illegally seized evidence, the Eleventh Circuit requires not only a reasonable probability that the evidence would have been found by lawful means, but also that the lawful means were possessed by the police and were being actively pursued before the illegal conduct occurred; because officers obtained the search warrant only hours after the warrantless search, the shotgun was not admissible under inevitable discovery — though its erroneous admission was harmless in light of the overwhelming other evidence.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Satterfield.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:123:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-warshak--181032\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Warshak\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Warshak\", \"cite\": \"631 F.3d 266 (2010)\", \"year\": 2010, \"court\": \"U.S. Court of Appeals, 6th Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 6th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/181032/united-states-v-warshak/\", \"holding\": \"A subscriber has a reasonable expectation of privacy in the contents of emails stored with, sent, or received through a commercial ISP; the government therefore may not compel an ISP to turn over the contents of a subscriber's emails without first obtaining a warrant based on probable cause, and to the extent the Stored Communications Act permits warrantless compelled disclosure of email contents it is unconstitutional. Because the agents relied in good faith on the SCA, however, the exclusionary rule did not require suppression.\", \"leg\": \"frontier\", \"prong\": \"b\", \"basis\": \"frontier-controlling\", \"homes\": [\"searches/the-third-party-doctrine-and-digital-surveillance/index.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"searches/the-third-party-doctrine-and-digital-surveillance/index.md\", \"role\": \"Anchor\"}], \"primary_home\": \"The Third Party Doctrine And Digital Surveillance\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"searches/the-third-party-doctrine-and-digital-surveillance/index.md\", \"role\": \"Anchor\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Warshak\", \"cite\": \"631 F.3d 266 (2010)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"A subscriber has a reasonable expectation of privacy in the contents of emails stored with, sent, or received through a commercial ISP; the government therefore may not compel an ISP to turn over the contents of a subscriber's emails without first obtaining a warrant based on probable cause, and to the extent the Stored Communications Act permits warrantless compelled disclosure of email contents it is unconstitutional.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Warshak.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:124:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-robinson-4th-cir-en-banc--4340460\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Robinson (4th Cir. en banc)\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Robinson (4th Cir. en banc)\", \"cite\": \"846 F.3d 694 (2017)\", \"year\": 2017, \"court\": \"U.S. Court of Appeals, 4th Cir. (en banc)\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 4th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4340460/united-states-v-shaquille-robinson/\", \"holding\": \"An officer who makes a lawful traffic stop and who reasonably suspects that one of the vehicle's occupants is armed may frisk that person for weapons without separately establishing that the person is dangerous, even where state law would allow the person to carry a concealed firearm; the danger justifying a protective frisk arises from the combination of a forced police encounter and the presence of a weapon, not from any illegality in the weapon's possession.\", \"leg\": \"frontier\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"frontier-split\", \"homes\": [\"seizures/Terry Stops and Reasonable Suspicion.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/Terry Stops and Reasonable Suspicion.md\", \"role\": \"Illustrates a circuit split\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Terry Stops and Reasonable Suspicion\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/Terry Stops and Reasonable Suspicion.md\", \"role\": \"Illustrates a circuit split\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Robinson (4th Cir. en banc)\", \"cite\": \"846 F.3d 694 (2017)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"An officer who makes a lawful traffic stop and who reasonably suspects that one of the vehicle's occupants is armed may frisk that person for weapons without separately establishing that the person is dangerous, even where state law would allow the person to carry a concealed firearm; the danger justifying a protective frisk arises from the combination of a forced police encounter and the presence of a weapon, not from any illegality in the weapon's possession.\"}], \"worklist_note\": null, \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Robinson (4th Cir. en banc).md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:126:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"state-v-larson--1187724\", \"record_id_after\": \"State v. Larson\", \"caption\": \"State v. Larson\", \"cite\": \"159 Or. App. 34 (1999)\", \"year\": 1999, \"court\": \"Or. Ct. App.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Persuasive — state, illustrative\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/1187724/state-v-larson/\", \"holding\": \"An apartment dweller can hold a protected privacy interest in a shared common area outside the unit; rather than mechanically applying single-family curtilage factors, a court evaluates the physical layout and the residents' use of the area — and because officers entered a partially enclosed strip behind the building to smell marijuana from the defendant's window, they invaded his protected privacy interest, so the resulting warrant evidence was suppressed.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"searches/Curtilage.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"searches/Curtilage.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Curtilage\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"searches/Curtilage.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"State v. Larson\", \"cite\": \"159 Or. App. 34 (1999)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"An apartment dweller can hold a protected privacy interest in a shared common area outside the unit; rather than mechanically applying single-family curtilage factors, a court evaluates the physical layout and the residents' use of the area — and because officers entered a partially enclosed strip behind the building to smell marijuana from the defendant's window, they invaded his protected privacy interest, so the resulting warrant evidence was suppressed.\"}], \"worklist_note\": \"2026-07-07 orchestrator remap-2 post-re-key: state-v-larson--10657314 -> state-v-larson--1187724 (pre-W5 audit session)\", \"page\": \"content/cases/State v. Larson.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:127:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-mendoza--10771114\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Mendoza\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Mendoza\", \"cite\": \"No. 25-1154, slip op. (3d Cir. 2026)\", \"year\": 2026, \"court\": \"3d Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 3d Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/10771114/united-states-v-ryan-mendoza/\", \"holding\": \"A hotel guest's reasonable expectation of privacy in his room ends when his rental period lapses and possession reverts to the hotel; because police searched the room roughly five hours after the noon checkout time — after the guest had failed to check out, his keycard had been deactivated, and the room had been marked vacant — Mendoza lacked a legitimate expectation of privacy and therefore had no standing to challenge the warrantless search.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"the-exclusionary-rule-remedies-and-standing/Standing to Challenge a Search.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"the-exclusionary-rule-remedies-and-standing/Standing to Challenge a Search.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Standing to Challenge a Search\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"the-exclusionary-rule-remedies-and-standing/Standing to Challenge a Search.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Mendoza\", \"cite\": \"No. 25-1154, slip op. (3d Cir. 2026)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"A hotel guest's reasonable expectation of privacy in his room ends when his rental period lapses and possession reverts to the hotel; because police searched the room roughly five hours after the noon checkout time — after the guest had failed to check out, his keycard had been deactivated, and the room had been marked vacant — Mendoza lacked a legitimate expectation of privacy and therefore had no standing to challenge the warrantless search.\"}], \"worklist_note\": \"2026-07-07 orchestrator remap-2 post-re-key: united-states-v-mendoza--10131439 -> united-states-v-mendoza--10771114 (pre-W5 audit session)\", \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Mendoza.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:128:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-cole--5307612\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Cole\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Cole\", \"cite\": \"21 F.4th 421 (2021)\", \"year\": 2021, \"court\": \"7th Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 7th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/5307612/united-states-v-janhoi-cole/\", \"holding\": \"Travel-plan questions ordinarily fall within the mission of a traffic stop and so do not, by themselves, measurably prolong it; like any inquiry during a stop, however, they must be reasonable under the circumstances — and here the trooper's follow-up questions were justified by the driver's evasive answers, and reasonable suspicion of drug trafficking developed before the stop was extended for a dog sniff, so suppression was properly denied.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"seizures/Traffic Stops.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/Traffic Stops.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Traffic Stops\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/Traffic Stops.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Cole\", \"cite\": \"21 F.4th 421 (2021)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"Travel-plan questions ordinarily fall within the mission of a traffic stop and so do not, by themselves, measurably prolong it; like any inquiry during a stop, however, they must be reasonable under the circumstances — and here the trooper's follow-up questions were justified by the driver's evasive answers, and reasonable suspicion of drug trafficking developed before the stop was extended for a dog sniff, so suppression was properly denied.\"}], \"worklist_note\": \"2026-07-07 orchestrator remap-2 post-re-key: united-states-v-cole--9623101 -> united-states-v-cole--5307612 (pre-W5 audit session)\", \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Cole.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:129:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-trent--10855903\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Trent\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Trent\", \"cite\": \"No. 25-5770, slip op. (6th Cir. 2026)\", \"year\": 2026, \"court\": \"6th Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 6th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/10855903/united-states-v-mark-anthony-trent/\", \"holding\": \"Under the collective-knowledge doctrine, the reasonable suspicion needed to prolong a completed traffic stop for a dog sniff may be imputed to the stopping officer from the knowledge of the investigating agents, even if the responding officer was unaware of the specific facts; because agents surveilling a methamphetamine 'turn-and-burn' operation had ample suspicion and the extension lasted only a minute or two, the brief prolongation was lawful and suppression was properly denied.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"seizures/Collective Knowledge and the Fellow-Officer Rule.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/Collective Knowledge and the Fellow-Officer Rule.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Collective Knowledge and the Fellow-Officer Rule\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/Collective Knowledge and the Fellow-Officer Rule.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Trent\", \"cite\": \"No. 25-5770, slip op. (6th Cir. 2026)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"Under the collective-knowledge doctrine, the reasonable suspicion needed to prolong a completed traffic stop for a dog sniff may be imputed to the stopping officer from the knowledge of the investigating agents, even if the responding officer was unaware of the specific facts; because agents surveilling a methamphetamine 'turn-and-burn' operation had ample suspicion and the extension lasted only a minute or two, the brief prolongation was lawful and suppression was properly denied.\"}], \"worklist_note\": \"2026-07-07 orchestrator remap-2 post-re-key: united-states-v-trent--4880705 -> united-states-v-trent--10855903 (pre-W5 audit session)\", \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Trent.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:131:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-lewis--9424185\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Lewis\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Lewis\", \"cite\": \"No. 22-5593, slip op. (6th Cir. 2023)\", \"year\": 2023, \"court\": \"U.S. Court of Appeals, 6th Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 6th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/9424185/united-states-v-edward-leonidas-lewis/\", \"holding\": \"A homeowner's consent to an on-site 'preview' of his laptop and cell phone authorized only that limited search; the later seizure and full forensic examination of the devices required a warrant, and because the supporting affidavit was a bare-bones, conclusory statement that recited only that a consented search had 'become apparent' incriminating — without any facts a magistrate could independently weigh — it failed to establish probable cause, and the good-faith exception could not save so deficient a warrant.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"warrant-exceptions/Consent Searches.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/Consent Searches.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Consent Searches\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/Consent Searches.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Lewis\", \"cite\": \"No. 22-5593, slip op. (6th Cir. 2023)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"A homeowner's consent to an on-site 'preview' of his laptop and cell phone authorized only that limited search; the later seizure and full forensic examination of the devices required a warrant, and because the supporting affidavit was a bare-bones, conclusory statement that recited only that a consented search had 'become apparent' incriminating — without any facts a magistrate could independently weigh — it failed to establish probable cause, and the good-faith exception could not save so deficient a warrant.\"}], \"worklist_note\": \"2026-07-07 orchestrator remap post-re-key: united-states-v-lewis--10640348 -> united-states-v-lewis--9424185 (consolidated-repair session)\", \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Lewis.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:133:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-porter--10810059\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Porter\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Porter\", \"cite\": \"No. 25-60163, slip op. (5th Cir. 2026)\", \"year\": 2026, \"court\": \"5th Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 5th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/10810059/united-states-v-porter/\", \"holding\": \"A police officer's use of a fixed license plate reader (LPR) to detect that a vehicle passed a public intersection is not a Fourth Amendment search and requires no warrant; the ensuing traffic stop was supported by reasonable suspicion, the officer lawfully seized a firearm and machinegun conversion switch he saw in plain view, and circuit precedent foreclosed the Second Amendment challenge, so the machinegun conviction was affirmed.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"searches/the-third-party-doctrine-and-digital-surveillance/index.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"searches/the-third-party-doctrine-and-digital-surveillance/index.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"The Third Party Doctrine And Digital Surveillance\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"searches/the-third-party-doctrine-and-digital-surveillance/index.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Porter\", \"cite\": \"No. 25-60163, slip op. (5th Cir. 2026)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"A police officer's use of a fixed license plate reader (LPR) to detect that a vehicle passed a public intersection is not a Fourth Amendment search and requires no warrant; the ensuing traffic stop was supported by reasonable suspicion, the officer lawfully seized a firearm and machinegun conversion switch he saw in plain view, and circuit precedent foreclosed the Second Amendment challenge, so the machinegun conviction was affirmed.\"}], \"worklist_note\": \"2026-07-07 orchestrator remap-2 post-re-key: united-states-v-porter--10626686 -> united-states-v-porter--10810059 (pre-W5 audit session)\", \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Porter.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:137:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"robinson-v-commonwealth--10838748\", \"record_id_after\": \"Robinson v. Commonwealth\", \"caption\": \"Robinson v. Commonwealth\", \"cite\": \"No. 1912-24-1, slip op. (Virginia 2026)\", \"year\": 2026, \"court\": \"Va. Ct. App.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Persuasive — state, illustrative\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/10838748/eddie-eugene-robinson-v-commonwealth-of-virginia/\", \"holding\": \"A police query of a network of fixed automatic license plate reader (Flock) cameras that photograph plates and vehicle exteriors on public roads and retain the data for 30 days is not a Fourth Amendment search on this record, because the system captures only a vehicle's public movements and does not create the kind of comprehensive chronicle of a person's life that Carpenter found to invade a reasonable expectation of privacy; the warrantless database query therefore required no warrant.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"searches/the-third-party-doctrine-and-digital-surveillance/index.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"searches/the-third-party-doctrine-and-digital-surveillance/index.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"The Third Party Doctrine And Digital Surveillance\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"searches/the-third-party-doctrine-and-digital-surveillance/index.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"Robinson v. Commonwealth\", \"cite\": \"No. 1912-24-1, slip op. (Virginia 2026)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"A police query of a network of fixed automatic license plate reader (Flock) cameras that photograph plates and vehicle exteriors on public roads and retain the data for 30 days is not a Fourth Amendment search on this record, because the system captures only a vehicle's public movements and does not create the kind of comprehensive chronicle of a person's life that Carpenter found to invade a reasonable expectation of privacy; the warrantless database query therefore required no warrant.\"}], \"worklist_note\": \"2026-07-07 orchestrator remap-2 post-re-key: robinson-v-commonwealth--10793178 -> robinson-v-commonwealth--10838748 (pre-W5 audit session)\", \"page\": \"content/cases/Robinson v. Commonwealth.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:138:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"people-v-frederick--4396951\", \"record_id_after\": \"People v. Frederick\", \"caption\": \"People v. Frederick\", \"cite\": \"500 Mich. 228 (2017)\", \"year\": 2017, \"court\": \"Mich.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Persuasive — state, illustrative\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4396951/people-of-michigan-v-michael-christopher-frederick/\", \"holding\": \"The implied license that lets an officer approach a home and knock is time-sensitive and generally does not extend to predawn approaches; when officers conducted 4:00 and 5:30 a.m. 'knock and talks' at the defendants' homes, they exceeded that license and trespassed on Fourth-Amendment-protected property, and because the trespass was joined to information-gathering it was a search — so the consents that followed had to be analyzed for taint from the illegal search.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"warrant-exceptions/Knock and Talk.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/Knock and Talk.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Knock and Talk\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/Knock and Talk.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"People v. Frederick\", \"cite\": \"500 Mich. 228 (2017)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"The implied license that lets an officer approach a home and knock is time-sensitive and generally does not extend to predawn approaches; when officers conducted 4:00 and 5:30 a.m.\"}], \"worklist_note\": \"2026-07-07 orchestrator remap-2 post-re-key: people-v-frederick--10579458 -> people-v-frederick--4396951 (pre-W5 audit session)\", \"page\": \"content/cases/People v. Frederick.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:141:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-chavez--171034\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Chavez\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Chavez\", \"cite\": \"534 F.3d 1338 (2008)\", \"year\": 2008, \"court\": \"10th Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 10th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/171034/united-states-v-chavez/\", \"holding\": \"Under the collective-knowledge doctrine, probable cause held by the DEA task force that investigated and arranged a controlled cocaine buy could be imputed to the patrolman the task force directed to stop the suspect's vehicle, even though the patrolman himself was not privy to the investigation's details; the stop and search were therefore justified and suppression was properly denied.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"seizures/Collective Knowledge and the Fellow-Officer Rule.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/Collective Knowledge and the Fellow-Officer Rule.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Collective Knowledge and the Fellow-Officer Rule\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"seizures/Collective Knowledge and the Fellow-Officer Rule.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Chavez\", \"cite\": \"534 F.3d 1338 (2008)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"Under the collective-knowledge doctrine, probable cause held by the DEA task force that investigated and arranged a controlled cocaine buy could be imputed to the patrolman the task force directed to stop the suspect's vehicle, even though the patrolman himself was not privy to the investigation's details; the stop and search were therefore justified and suppression was properly denied.\"}], \"worklist_note\": \"2026-07-07 orchestrator remap-2 post-re-key: united-states-v-chavez--10329331 -> united-states-v-chavez--171034 (pre-W5 audit session)\", \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Chavez.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:142:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-crumble--4456532\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Crumble\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Crumble\", \"cite\": \"878 F.3d 656 (2018)\", \"year\": 2018, \"court\": \"8th Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 8th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4456532/united-states-v-prentiss-anthony-crumble/\", \"holding\": \"A person who flees a wrecked car and leaves his cell phone behind, then denies any knowledge of the vehicle, abandons the phone and forfeits any reasonable expectation of privacy in it — judged by the objective facts available to officers, not the owner's subjective intent — and the abandonment doctrine applies to cell phones notwithstanding Riley v. California, so the warrantless seizure and later search of the phone did not violate the Fourth Amendment.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"searches/Abandonment.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"searches/Abandonment.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Abandonment\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"searches/Abandonment.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Crumble\", \"cite\": \"878 F.3d 656 (2018)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"A person who flees a wrecked car and leaves his cell phone behind, then denies any knowledge of the vehicle, abandons the phone and forfeits any reasonable expectation of privacy in it — judged by the objective facts available to officers, not the owner's subjective intent — and the abandonment doctrine applies to cell phones notwithstanding Riley v.\"}], \"worklist_note\": \"2026-07-07 orchestrator remap-2 post-re-key: united-states-v-crumble--4767477 -> united-states-v-crumble--4456532 (pre-W5 audit session)\", \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Crumble.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:143:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-burgess--172511\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Burgess\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Burgess\", \"cite\": \"576 F.3d 1078 (2009)\", \"year\": 2009, \"court\": \"10th Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 10th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/172511/united-states-v-burgess/\", \"holding\": \"A warrant to search a vehicle for evidence of drug trafficking that authorizes a search of 'computer records,' read together with its supporting affidavit, is sufficiently particular and limits the computer search to the kind of drug-trafficking evidence likely to be found on the device; officers who came upon child pornography while conducting a scope-limited forensic search of seized hard drives did not exceed the warrant, and suppression was properly denied.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"searches/Plain View Doctrine.md\", \"warrant-exceptions/programmatic-and-special-needs-searches/Border Searches.md\"], \"roles\": [{\"home\": \"searches/Plain View Doctrine.md\", \"role\": \"Key\"}, {\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/programmatic-and-special-needs-searches/Border Searches.md\", \"role\": \"Related (cross-doctrine)\"}], \"primary_home\": \"Plain View Doctrine\", \"home_rows\": [{\"home\": \"searches/Plain View Doctrine.md\", \"role\": \"Key\", \"role_class\": \"key\", \"schema\": \"key\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Burgess\", \"cite\": \"576 F.3d 1078 (2009)\", \"holding_cell_hint\": \"A warrant to search a vehicle for evidence of drug trafficking that authorizes a search of 'computer records,' read together with its supporting affidavit, is sufficiently particular and limits the computer search to the kind of drug-trafficking evidence likely to be found on the device; officers who came upon child pornography while conducting a scope-limited forensic search of seized hard drives did not exceed the warrant, and suppression was properly denied.\"}, {\"home\": \"warrant-exceptions/programmatic-and-special-needs-searches/Border Searches.md\", \"role\": \"Related (cross-doctrine)\", \"role_class\": \"related\", \"schema\": \"related\", \"stem\": \"United States v. Burgess\", \"cite\": \"576 F.3d 1078 (2009)\", \"relevance_tag_hint\": null, \"relevance_cell_hint\": null, \"primary_home\": \"Plain View Doctrine\"}], \"worklist_note\": \"2026-07-07 orchestrator remap-2 post-re-key: united-states-v-burgess--9495745 -> united-states-v-burgess--172511 (pre-W5 audit session)\", \"page\": \"content/cases/United States v. Burgess.md\", \"born_status\": \"under_review\", \"lints_run\": [\"LINT-15\", \"LINT-16\", \"LINT-14\"], \"as_of\": \"2026-07-07\", \"ts\": \"2026-07-07\", \"lane\": \"s6-r8-mint\", \"model\": \"claude-opus-4-8\"}\n_run/o2-execute/s6-authored-ledger.jsonl:144:{\"terminal\": \"authored\", \"record_id_before\": \"united-states-v-castillo--9407477\", \"record_id_after\": \"United States v. Castillo\", \"caption\": \"United States v. Castillo\", \"cite\": \"70 F.4th 894 (2023)\", \"year\": 2023, \"court\": \"5th Cir.\", \"authority_weight\": \"Binding in-circuit — 5th Cir.\", \"opinion_url\": \"https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/9407477/united-states-v-castillo/\", \"holding\": \"Joining every circuit to have addressed the question, the Fifth Circuit held that no individualized suspicion is required for the government to conduct a manual border search of a cell phone; because agents at a port of entry manually scrolled through Castillo's phone and found child pornography, the search was reasonable by virtue of occurring at the border, and suppression was properly denied.\", \"leg\": \"roster\", \"prong\": \"a\", \"basis\": \"D1-flip\", \"homes\": [\"warrant-exceptions/programmatic-and-special-needs-searches/Border Searches.md\"],